/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188458 #edb/ref 1050 780.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188458 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "4306f994-2576-5ea8-aa86-53b75b9d993b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188535 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186185490 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188535 #edb/ref 1069 "q-110390" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188535 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$f_{X}(x) = {2x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwisef_{Y}(y) = {3y,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le y \\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind $P(X \\le 0.5, Y \\le \\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}})$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188535 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0111779d-e047-5622-b502-1438439ba7f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188535 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188561 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186185646 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188561 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143352" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188561 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be two independent continuous random variables whose marginal density functions are as follows:\n\n$f_{X}(x) = {\\frac{3}{2}\\sqrt{x},\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 1, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwisef_{Y}(y) = {\\frac{2}{3}y,\\begin{vmatrix}1 \\le y \\le 2, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\n\nFind $P(0 \\le X \\le 0.4, Y \\le 2)$. Round your answer to three decimal places.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188561 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "01f303ca-e9ff-5d45-9ae0-7430f5f2b5fe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186188561 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189079 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189079 #edb/ref 1061 34269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189079 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "57744d65-db5a-5f3d-bf13-43a012006fdf" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189079 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188274 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189080 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189113 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189080 #edb/ref 1061 34272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189080 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "36904357-d45f-58d8-af71-db1903bb3b44" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189080 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189113 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189113 #edb/ref 1061 38081 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189113 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9b164372-2e9b-5b11-9a7e-f7aa6897c577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189113 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186188295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189114 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186189079 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189114 #edb/ref 1061 38083 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189114 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "074fbae0-d9f5-5e1f-8944-2352a5ff16a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189114 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189221 #edb/ref 1061 39479 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189221 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "260c1a0b-a4ec-5618-9cb3-1bfc17d53a21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189221 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186189419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189379 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be continuous random variables. We say that $X$ and $Y$ are **independent** if, for all $A\\subseteq \\mathbb R$ and $B\\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(X\\in A, Y\\in B)=P(X\\in A)\\cdot P(Y\\in B).\n$$\n\nIn practice, this is not a very helpful definition when checking for independence since it involves verifying that it's true for all possible subsets $A$ and $B!$\n\nNow suppose that $X$ and $Y$ have the joint probability density function $f(x,y),$ and $f_X(x)$ and $f_Y(y)$ are the marginal density functions of $X$ and $Y,$ respectively. Then, it can be shown that $X$ and $Y$ are independent if and only if\n\n$$\nf(x, y) = f_X(x)\\cdot f_Y (y)\n$$\n\n \n\nfor all values of $x$ and $y.$ Notice that this has a direct analogy with the case of independence for discrete random variables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189379 #edb/ref 1065 6283 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189379 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "c151ba98-beac-55c8-9a33-b3f619f0a290" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189379 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189419 #edb/ref 1064 "For continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ whose joint support $S$ is rectangular, we have the following theorem:\n\n*Suppose that the joint support of two continuous random variables $X$ and $Y$ with a joint probability density function $f(x,y)$ is rectangular. If \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = g(x)\\cdot h(y)\n$$\n\nfor some two functions $g$ and $h$ (not necessarily probability density functions), then $X$ and $Y$ are independent. Note that the rectangular joint support $S$ does not need to be finite.* \n\nFor example, let's consider the random variables $X$ and $Y$ with the following joint PDF:\n\n![](images/01/01a68d0e42bc71729543e74722390291787ce60530c2432ffa0cdf6c0bb5f093.svg)\n\nThe joint support $[0,2]\\times [0, \\infty)$ is rectangular. Also, we can write $f(x,y) = g(x)\\cdot h(y),$ where\n\n$$\ng(x) = \\dfrac14 x, \\qquad h(y) = e^{-y/2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $X$ and $Y$ are independent." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189419 #edb/ref 1065 6534 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189419 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9e6987fa-0133-5b85-b6a5-e58a401020b9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186189419 #edb/ref 1067 "Independence When the Joint Support Is Rectangular" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
